soc: mediatek: mt6797-a72: hand the cluster's frequency to cpufreq

This driver has owned the A72 clock since the cluster first came up, because
nothing else could. clk/0003 gives the clock a real provider and dts/0061-0062
describe the cluster to mediatek-cpufreq, so it does not have to any more --
and must not, because two writers on one clock and one rail is how this
cluster gets hung.

WHAT MOVES. mt6797_a72_retune() keeps its name and its sequence -- park on the
mainpll backup, ask the firmware to move the PLL, come back -- but every step
is now issued through the clock framework instead of by hand. The
BIGIDVFSPLLSETFREQ call, the CSPM semaphore around it and the Vsram raise that
has to precede any clock above 1 GHz all live in clk-mt6797-mcumixed.c now,
which is where the mux register is and where the A53 clusters' half of it is
already written under a lock. What is left here is bring-up: power, the
prerequisites, PSCI, and the rail.

WHAT STANDS ASIDE. a72_cpufreq_managed is read from the same property
mediatek-cpufreq reads -- operating-points-v2 on the cpu8 node -- so the two
can never disagree about who is in charge. When it is set, the
raise-to-default work does not raise anything and the freq_mhz sysfs knob is
read-only, pointing at policy8. The knob's old "requests above the table run
on the top voltage" behaviour goes with it; the ceiling is now the OPP table's
and raising it is issue #59's decision, not a sysfs write's.

THE RAIL, AND WHY THE SHAPE OF A REGULATOR REQUEST MATTERS. The regulator core
aggregates consumers by INTERSECTION -- highest min_uV, lowest max_uV, -EINVAL
if what is left is empty -- so a consumer pinning [1140000, 1140000] does not
"ask for 1140 mV", it forbids everyone else from asking for anything else.
mediatek-cpufreq asks for [vproc, proc_max_volt], open at the top, which is
what makes a floor from this driver compose with it.

So: bring up at the proven 1140 mV as a range open at the top, and once CPU_ON
has succeeded drop the floor to 880 mV -- the bottom of this cluster's own OPP
table -- and leave it there. cpufreq can then reach every point in its table,
and the rail still cannot go below the lowest voltage the vendor specifies for
these cores, whatever a governor decides. Both orderings are safe if cpufreq's
policy appears before the work runs: the aggregate is max(880000, whatever
cpufreq asked for), and cpufreq raises voltage before it raises the clock.

Also recorded here, because chasing it is what turned up issue #84: step 5 of
the bring-up programs ARMCAXPLL2, and ARMCAXPLL2 is the CCI's PLL, not this
cluster's. The vendor maps LL/L/B/CCI to ARMCAXPLL0/1/NULL/2, and it is
measurable -- with the A72s at 1989 MHz, ARMCAXPLL2_CON1 still decodes to
750 MHz. Unchanged here; only written down.

THREE THINGS THE FRAMEWORK DOES NOT DO FOR US, ADDED AFTER REVIEW.

The intermediate clock is now clk_prepare_enable()d and held. armpll_div_pll1
is a real gate (CLK_MISC_CFG_0 bit 4) and the open-coded retune used to set it
by hand before parking. Driving the mux through the framework means the
framework has to be told the gate is needed, by a consumer that is going to
park on it -- relying on an A53 policy happening to hold it is not a reference,
and it is exactly absent on the path this file still drives itself.

a72_clks_acquire() publishes a72_cpu_clk last, under a mutex, because
a72_cpu_clk is the flag as well as the handle and a72_cur_mhz() reads it from
sysfs with nothing else held. Seeing it set while a72_inter_clk was still NULL
would put clk_set_parent(cpu_clk, NULL) into the next retune, and the clock
core turns a NULL parent into index 0 -- which on this mux is the 26 MHz
oscillator, under two running A72s.

mt6797_a72_prepare() now reads the cluster clock's rate while the cluster is
still down, which is what makes the clock driver forget a rate cached from
before a power cycle. The firmware re-parks the PLL at 750 MHz on every
power-up; without this, clk_set_rate() back to the old value short-circuits on
the cache and does nothing, leaving the cores at 750 MHz while cpufreq, the
scheduler capacity and freq_mhz all report the old figure on the voltage that
figure implies.

AND ONE THING THAT IS NOW WEAKER, DELIBERATELY. The CSPM semaphore covers the
firmware call but no longer spans the mux park and unpark either side of it,
because those are two separate clock-framework operations now. The exposure
that buys is a CPU_ON racing a governor transition: the firmware moves
MUXSEL[1:0] as part of powering the cluster, and doing that mid-PLL-change is
the hang the park exists to prevent. It does not arise on this board's
sequence -- the firmware only touches the cluster clock when it raises the
CLUSTER, i.e. for the first core, and there is no policy to transition until
that core is online; the second core's CPU_ON goes straight to the core, as
mt6797_a72_prepare() already documents. Written down because it is a real
narrowing of a guarantee, not because it is reachable here.

